    {
      "id": 1,
      "title": "Statistical Significance ≠ Business Significance",
      "category": "Foundational Concept",
      "one_sentence_summary": "A p-value tells you if an effect exists; it doesn't tell you if the effect matters for your business goals.",
      "farmburg_example": "Group A had the highest conversion (19%) and highly significant chi-square (p<1e-30), yet FAILED to meet the $1,000/week revenue target. Group C had the lowest conversion (6%) but WAS the only group to pass the revenue threshold test.",
      "why_it_matters": "Optimizing for the wrong metric (conversion vs revenue) leads to suboptimal business decisions. The statistical technique must match the business question.",
      "code_snippet": "# WRONG question: Which group converts most?\nchi2, p, _, _ = chi2_contingency(crosstab(group, purchase))\n# Result: Group A 'wins'\n\n# RIGHT question: Which group exceeds revenue target?\nfor g in ['A', 'B', 'C']:\n    p_value = binom_test(sales[g], n=n[g], p=target_rate[g], alt='greater')\n    # Result: Only Group C passes",
      "takeaway": "Before running any test, write down the specific business question. Select the statistical method that directly answers THAT question, not the one you're familiar with."
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "title": "Multiple Testing Inflates False Positives",
      "category": "Methodological Rigor",
      "one_sentence_summary": "Running multiple tests without correction makes it increasingly likely you'll find a 'significant' result by chance alone.",
      "farmburg_example": "With 3 binomial tests at α=0.05, the family-wise error rate is 14.3% (not 5%). Without Bonferroni correction (α=0.0167), we risk implementing pricing based on statistical noise.",
      "why_it_matters": "False discoveries lead to costly missteps. Correction methods protect against spurious findings when running multiple comparisons.",
      "code_snippet": "from itertools import combinations\nn_tests = 3\nalpha_bonferroni = 0.05 / n_tests  # 0.0167\n\n# For pairwise comparisons\nfor g1, g2 in combinations(['A', 'B', 'C'], 2):\n    chi2, p, _, _ = chi2_contingency(subtable)\n    if p < alpha_bonferroni:\n        print(f'{g1} vs {g2}: SIGNIFICANT')",
      "takeaway": "Always apply corrections (Bonferroni, Benjamini-Hochberg) when running multiple tests. Pre-register the number of planned comparisons to avoid p-hacking."
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "title": "Confidence Intervals Tell the Full Story",
      "category": "Communication & Reporting",
      "one_sentence_summary": "P-values give binary answers; CIs show magnitude, direction, and uncertainty simultaneously.",
      "farmburg_example": "Group C's CI [5.02%, 7.31%] sat ENTIRELY above the 4.01% target (strong evidence). Group A's CI [17.17%, 20.92%] straddled 20.21% (genuine uncertainty).",
      "why_it_matters": "CIs enable stakeholders to assess risk. A point estimate hides whether the result could reasonably be below or above thresholds.",
      "code_snippet": "ci_low, ci_high = proportion_confint(\n    count=sales, nobs=n, alpha=0.05, method='wilson'\n)\n\nif ci_low > target_rate:\n    print('STRONG EVIDENCE: Entire CI above target')\nelif ci_high < target_rate:\n    print('FAILS: Entire CI below target')\nelse:\n    print('UNCERTAIN: CI straddles target')",
      "takeaway": "Always report CIs alongside p-values. For executives, visualize CIs with threshold lines—shading regions above/below targets makes uncertainty intuitive."
    },
    {
