      "title": "Sample Size Determines Detectability",
      "category": "Experimental Design",
      "one_sentence_summary": "Underpowered tests can never confirm the null hypothesis—they can only fail to reject it due to insufficient data.",
      "farmburg_example": "With n=1,666 per group, we had 88% power to detect small effects. Had we used n=500, power would drop to 49%—a coin flip whether we'd miss real differences.",
      "why_it_matters": "A non-significant result with low power is uninterpretable. It tells us nothing about whether effects exist.",
      "code_snippet": "from statsmodels.stats.power import NormalIndPower\nanalysis = NormalIndPower()\n\n# What's the minimum detectable effect?\nmde = analysis.solve_power(nobs1=1666, alpha=0.05, power=0.80)\nprint(f'MDE at 80% power: Cohen\\'s h = {mde:.3f}')\n\n# Power curve\nfor n in [100, 500, 1000, 1666, 5000]:\n    power = analysis.solve_power(effect_size=0.1, nobs1=n, alpha=0.05)\n    print(f'n={n}: power={power*100:.0f}%')",
      "takeaway": "Calculate sample size requirements BEFORE launching tests using power analysis. Base the calculation on the MINIMUM effect size that matters for business—not on arbitrary guesses."
